[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked *known: one issue, one fix queued*. Its reports describe
three different problems. Each one needs its own fix, so each one now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix ends only the <code>parser</code> reports. The <code>hint</code> and
<code>one-day</code> reports keep coming after it ships.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

Every report is counted once. The export repeats some reports across its pages, and the table
counts each report id once (`uniqueById`).

## parser: the shared parser reads dates month first

Reports: <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" />. Every tenant on file writes the day
first. The shared parser reads `07/04` as 4 July, so day and month swap: *"Customer booked 7
April and got an invite for 4 July."* This happens in every channel and at every tenant on file. It is
the bug the #4471 page describes, and the queued fix is for this one.

## hint: booking-v3 prints a month-first hint and reads the day first

Reports: <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" />. They come in through the booking form at the
tenants on `booking-v3`. Under the date field that form prints `MM/DD/YYYY`. People type what the
hint tells them, month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The swap looks the
same as `parser`, but the parser is not involved, so the parser fix does not end it. The fix is
the hint, or the form reading what the hint asks for.

Intake tagged these reports by the reporter's browser, not by the tenant's form. Intake's
`hint` tag and this group do not match, so do not use the tag to triage this problem.

## one-day: the date moves by one day

Reports: <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="one-day" />. Here day and month do not swap. The date moves by
one day: *"Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting."* Others say
the invite says Tuesday and the calendar says Monday, that only June is one day off, and that the
invite arrived before the booking was made. That points at time zones, not at the date format, so
neither the parser fix nor the hint fix ends it. The desk had already read these as a one-day move
(`desk-notes.json`), but #4471 still counted them under `parser`.

## Why one ticket hid three

The desk merged every date report into #4471 under one title. Decision
[0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone, and its consequences say
engineering splits a ticket later. Nobody split #4471, so one known fix covered three problems.
The table on this page counts by problem, from the reports themselves, at render time.

